<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Gavin's hardened runtime entitlements.

  Every key here is FALSE on purpose. The hardened runtime already denies
  each of these; naming them anyway is what makes a future widening a
  reviewable diff instead of an invisible default, and it is the record of
  the audit that produced the list. codesign embeds the file verbatim, and
  scripts/verify-macos-bundle.sh fails a build whose signed blob contains a
  <true/>.

  No "..." (double hyphen) anywhere in this comment, ever: XML forbids it
  inside a comment, and while `plutil -lint` happily accepts one, the
  parser behind codesign does not. It fails the whole bundle with
  "Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error".

  What gavin actually does, and why none of it needs an exception:

  * Spawns child processes: `gavin-daemon` and `gavin-mcp` beside the app
    binary, plus `git`, `security`, `curl`, `nc`, `pkill`, `/bin/sh` and
    whatever agent CLI a session runs. The hardened runtime does not gate
    `exec`; each child gets its own code identity and its own rules. The
    two sidecars ship inside the bundle and are signed with it, so they are
    covered by the same seal rather than by an entitlement.

  * Opens PTYs (`forkpty` in crates/daemon/src/pty.rs). An ordinary
    syscall, gated by no entitlement, sandbox or otherwise.

  * Reads the Claude Code OAuth token for the usage probes by shelling out
    to `/usr/bin/security find-generic-password` (app/src-tauri/src/
    agent_usage.rs). Keychain reads are governed by the item's ACL, not by
    an entitlement: `com.apple.security.keychain` exists only inside the
    App Sandbox, which gavin does not enter. The requesting binary is
    Apple's `security`, so the first read prompts the human however gavin
    is signed.

  * Reads and writes files anywhere the human points a workspace. No
    sandbox, so no file entitlements; the protected locations (Desktop,
    Documents, Downloads, iCloud) are TCC prompts, and a stable Developer
    ID identity is what makes those grants survive a rebuild.

  * Renders in WKWebView. WebKit runs JavaScript out of process, in
    Apple's own com.apple.WebKit.WebContent, which carries its own JIT
    entitlements. Unlike a Chromium host, the Tauri app needs none.

  * Talks to the network (the Anthropic usage endpoint, git remotes).
    Outbound network needs no entitlement outside the sandbox.

  * Sends notifications through tauri-plugin-notification, and moves a
    deleted workspace to the Trash through NSFileManager. Both want a
    signed bundle identity; neither wants an entitlement, and the
    NSFileManager route is deliberately not the Finder/AppleScript one, so
    there is no Apple events entitlement either.

  If a future change genuinely needs one of these, flip it here in its own
  commit and say in the message what stopped working without it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.debugger</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.get-task-allow</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
